  "notes": "The R&B/hip-hop-native end of country — spoken-sung verses, programmed beds, \"busy electronic arrangements and … fleet-footed rhythms.\" The tempo and key are documented outright rather than cross-folded: the signature single is \"in the key of F major with a tempo of 96–100 beats per minute,\" so the band centres at 98 with the slow cuts reaching down to 65. The drum grammar is the entry's list: \"a snap/clap on the backbeat, 16th hats with occasional 32nd rolls, a short sampled kick on 1 and the 'and-of-3,' finger-snaps and vocal-percussion layers; live drums are credited alongside 'programming' on the album, so model a hybrid where programming is the skeleton and live cymbals/toms are overdubs\" — that is a tight quantize with a real snap lane and 32nd rolls at the boundaries. The 808 takes `counter_riff` because both layers are named: \"sub-bass is structural — long, tuned, root-locked with occasional slides; the electric bass, where present, plays a syncopated R&B figure above the sub.\" Harmony is deliberately inert and the entry says so: \"I–V–vi–IV rendered as a two-bar loop rather than a four-bar progression, with sustained pads over a static bass — the harmony is deliberately less eventful than the rhythm,\" hence the `2_bars_per_chord` and `vamp` weighting over one dominant family. Melody is \"talk-singing at conversational syllable density in verses (spoken rhythm, minimal pitch movement), then a sung chorus with a short repeated hook cell; register mid-baritone; melisma absent.\" ⚠ Song length is a documented parameter — \"the signature single runs 2:40\" — so the arrangement sections are the shortest in this section. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the 808 register, the snap density and the counter ratio.",
